97 #edb/ref 1080 "0.078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccc836ed-053c-5a8d-a101-3f2f433a8018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156098 #edb/ref 1080 "0.101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156098 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156098 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e0c7737e-d54b-52cb-85ec-26caa774879e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186156101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156099 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4f1f73ff-7c4d-523b-9ef8-9d5369208ce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156100 #edb/ref 1080 "0.998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156100 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156100 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6ddae42-3314-596e-aa0d-6eef64561e80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156101 #edb/ref 1080 "0.970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156101 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156101 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbcee99b-8144-55b0-8281-1752aa31f008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156102 #edb/ref 1080 "0.952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05f87902-a160-56e3-b333-87b6b94e3920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156103 #edb/ref 1080 "0.915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156103 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156103 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b8063d0-77e3-50d5-8f69-4f962ccbea66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156104 #edb/ref 1080 "0.887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156104 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156104 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67dfd1d9-169a-5fae-85e6-43e81a239051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157112 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157112 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of calls received at a telephone center over a 10-minute period\n\n- The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\n- The number of pairs of shoes produced with defects in a factory in a single day" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ba74c8a-f3b2-5a1d-a596-9f41be61e7d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157112 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (10 minutes).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. The pairs of shoes produced with defects can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one day).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157113 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157113 #edb/ref 1071 "In a telephone exchange, an average of $3$ prank calls are received per day. What is the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls tomorrow? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that prank calls occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f9af894-7d9c-5535-8db7-ff928a5a290a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157113 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of prank calls received by the telephone exchange. Since the average rate is $3$ prank calls per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of prank calls can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 3,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{5}{e}^{-3}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157114 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157114 #edb/ref 1071 "On average, $4$ traffic accidents occur per week on a particular freeway. What is the probability of fewer than $3$ accidents next week? You may assume that accidents occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae98c2fc-bfb5-5f5d-bfce-f536aa42e5d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157114 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of traffic accidents. Since the average rate is $4$ traffic accidents per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of traffic accidents can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 4,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $3$ traffic accidents on the next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{0}{e}^{-4}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}{e}^{-4}}{2!} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4}(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2!}) \\\\  & ≈0.238\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157115 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157115 #edb/ref 1071 "In a factory, $3$ machines, on average, suffer some fault during the course of a week. What is the probability that at least $2$ machines will register a fault next week? You may assume that faults occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5be23338-73b5-50b5-8dc8-0aa6d264afb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157115 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of machines with a fault in the factory. Since the average rate is $3$ faulty machines per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of machines with faults can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 3,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\geq 2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = 1 - P(X<  2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{0}{e}^{-3}}{0!}+\\frac{{3}^{1}{e}^{-3}}{1!} \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of at least $2$ machines registering a fault next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.199 \\\\  & ≈0.801\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157308 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157309 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157310 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157311 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling With the Poisson Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157493 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0acc44f8-9b24-5d3b-9b0b-bdb2777ea7d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $4$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding fewer than $4$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41714e88-969e-5fd4-a843-bf3505bddd1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157860 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typos on a page of the book. Since the average rate is $4$ typos per page, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typos can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $4$ typos on the next page is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{0}{e}^{-4}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}{e}^{-4}}{2!}+\\frac{{4}^{3}{e}^{-4}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.433\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $2$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding at least $4$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22614c86-c023-5f35-9da5-8efbe37e0d26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157862 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typos on a page of the book. Since the average rate is $2$ typos per page, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typos can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =2,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(2).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2}^{x}{e}^{-2}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}{e}^{-2}}{0!}+\\frac{{2}^{1}{e}^{-2}}{1!}+\\frac{{2}^{2}{e}^{-2}}{2!}+\\frac{{2}^{3}{e}^{-2}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding at least $4$ typos on the next page is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.857 \\\\  & =0.143\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1071 "The manager at a water bottle factory notices that, on average, every batch of water bottles has $7$ water bottles on which the label is upside down. What is the probability that the next batch of water bottles will have more than $2$ bottles on which the label is upside down? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that upside-down labels occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae5e4edd-dca1-522d-a017-c3f07e4598a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157863 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of water bottles on which the label is upside down. Since the average rate is $7$ upside-down labels per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of upside-down labels can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.030\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ upside-down labels in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.030 \\\\  & =0.970\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158155 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158155 #edb/ref 1061 27837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "baff7f20-dc53-5d87-94c9-0725cca77ccb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158156 #edb/ref 1061 27839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a629aba-522f-548e-80da-577c194fdfe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158157 #edb/ref 1061 27840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b44c854-917d-5ba0-b5a2-d41308ca5256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1061 27841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61e344b0-9976-54f7-a03c-175fe1505fbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1061 27850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd4dd871-25f7-54f5-9345-a556f2356cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1061 27932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3576e13c-4960-5e5a-b072-2acaff8161cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1064 "A **Poisson distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nIf the rate of the events is $\\lambda,$ then the number of events $x$ occurring in an interval of fixed length has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a Poisson distribution with rate $\\lambda,$ then we write $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda).$\n\nFor example, suppose that calls come in at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute at a call center. Then the number of calls $X$ that are received during the next minute has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of receiving $4$ calls during the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{4}{e}^{-3}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1065 4525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e07ef85-f3ca-52c8-89e2-062d8ea7c9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1064 "The shape of the graph of the Poisson distribution depends on the value of $\\lambda.$ When $\\lambda$ is small, the probability is concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $\\lambda$ is larger, the probability is concentrated at larger values of $x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8af371733bd248a4e5ba3bc35199f00414188e9ca033a04dee2a7de4b0a7db4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1065 4534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175